{"title":"Kestrel Metro","sub":"Free buses, or more buses?","type":"Public sector","industry":"Public transport","geo":"United Kingdom","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Public sector · policy options · cost per outcome","prompt":"A city transport authority wants more people to take the bus instead of driving, to cut congestion and emissions. A popular proposal is to make buses free, costing $30M a year in lost fares. The authority could instead spend the same $30M running buses more often. You're asked which gets more new riders.","clarify":[{"q":"Ridership today?","a":"60 million trips a year; fares bring in $30M."},{"q":"Free fares?","a":"Studies of similar cities suggest about 12% more trips, but much of the increase comes from people who used to walk or cycle, not drive."},{"q":"More frequent buses?","a":"$30M a year would raise frequency on the 20 busiest routes from every 12 minutes to every 6; similar changes elsewhere raised trips across the whole network by about 18%."},{"q":"Equity?","a":"About 35% of riders are on low incomes."}],"structure":{"root":"Which option attracts more new riders for $30M a year?","branches":[{"head":"New trips","items":["Free fares","Higher frequency"]},{"head":"Cost per new trip","items":["Lost fares vs extra operating cost"]},{"head":"Quality of new trips","items":["From cars, or from walking and cycling?"]},{"head":"Fairness","items":["Help for low-income riders"]}]},"hypothesis":"Free fares spend most of the money on trips people already make. More frequent service attracts people who currently find the bus too slow, often drivers.","questions":[{"q":"Compare new trips and cost per new trip.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"392.644719pt\" height=\"169.072pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 392.644719 169.072\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g